01:49:20.610 --> 01:49:24.170
this is a celebration of a creative collaboration

3087
01:49:24.170 --> 01:49:26.010
with Ten Thousand.

3088
01:49:26.010 --> 01:49:27.180
I'm so proud of the kit.

3089
01:49:27.180 --> 01:49:28.390
How do you like the kit Robbie?

3090
01:49:28.390 --> 01:49:30.134
It's amazing.

3091
01:49:30.134 --> 01:49:31.311
You have to say that right?

3092
01:49:31.311 --> 01:49:32.870
(Rich laughing)
Like right there on the spot.

3093
01:49:32.870 --> 01:49:34.650
I am really proud of it.

3094
01:49:34.650 --> 01:49:37.720
It was really amazing to collaborate with such a talented

3095
01:49:37.720 --> 01:49:39.240
group of people.

3096
01:49:39.240 --> 01:49:41.840
I've never worked with a company that was more responsive

3097
01:49:41.840 --> 01:49:44.150
to athlete feedback.

3098
01:49:44.150 --> 01:49:47.040
The number of samples that I continue to get in the mail

3099
01:49:47.040 --> 01:49:49.400
over the course of a year was insane.

3100
01:49:49.400 --> 01:49:52.597
I couldn't believe how immersive it was as an experience

3101
01:49:52.597 --> 01:49:54.290
and it was really creatively fulfilling.

3102
01:49:54.290 --> 01:49:56.920
So I just wanna thank everybody at Ten Thousand,

3103
01:49:56.920 --> 01:50:00.280
Keith of course, Dominic, Travis, Brian,

3104
01:50:00.280 --> 01:50:03.860
everybody who collaborated on what we're here to celebrate

3105
01:50:03.860 --> 01:50:05.353
today, so thank you guys.

3106
01:50:06.420 --> 01:50:07.719
I'm really proud to be here.

3107
01:50:07.719 --> 01:50:10.802
(audience clapping)

3108
01:50:13.160 --> 01:50:16.190
I have the distinct honor of hosting a conversation

3109
01:50:16.190 --> 01:50:19.220
with my friend Robbie here, Robbie Ballenger.

3110
01:50:19.220 --> 01:50:22.020
Many of you probably know Robbie already.

3111
01:50:22.020 --> 01:50:26.770
He's distinguished as a extraordinary athlete

3112
01:50:26.770 --> 01:50:28.380
and ambassador of running.

3113
01:50:28.380 --> 01:50:30.960
Not only as he run across the United States,

3114
01:50:30.960 --> 01:50:35.960
he recently broke the 24 hour Central Park loop record.

3115
01:50:36.830 --> 01:50:38.121
Did I say that right?

3116
01:50:38.121 --> 01:50:39.780
I don't know what it's called exactly.

3117
01:50:39.780 --> 01:50:41.500
Central Park loop challenge.

3118
01:50:41.500 --> 01:50:44.040
Yeah. And he ran how many miles in Central Park?

3119
01:50:44.040 --> 01:50:46.240
Oh, it's actually, it's the hours it's open.

3120
01:50:46.240 --> 01:50:48.620
So it's from 6:00 AM until midnight is the time

3121
01:50:48.620 --> 01:50:51.080
you have to run, which is 19 hours.

3122
01:50:51.080 --> 01:50:54.510
I finished in 18 hours and seven minutes with 16 loops.

3123
01:50:54.510 --> 01:50:57.530
So it was 98 miles in some change.

3124
01:50:57.530 --> 01:50:59.470
Right. But then you, you made sure you tagged on

3125
01:50:59.470 --> 01:51:00.303
a few miles.

3126
01:51:00.303 --> 01:51:01.380
Yeah.

3127
01:51:01.380 --> 01:51:02.213
(indistinct chattering)

3128
01:51:02.213 --> 01:51:03.510
Like what's the point of getting that far

3129
01:51:03.510 --> 01:51:04.996
and not going to 100.

3130
01:51:04.996 --> 01:51:06.210
(all laughing)

3131
01:51:06.210 --> 01:51:07.970
Three digits always looks better that two.

3132
01:51:07.970 --> 01:51:11.313
So, 98.6 miles in 17-

3133
01:51:13.191 --> 01:51:15.030
18 hours and seven minutes.

3134
01:51:15.030 --> 01:51:16.830
So most of you, many of you I'm sure

3135
01:51:16.830 --> 01:51:20.140
have run the full, is it 6.2,

3136
01:51:20.140 --> 01:51:21.700
the full Central Park loop, right?

3137
01:51:21.700 --> 01:51:24.300
Raise your hand if you've done that loop.

3138
01:51:24.300 --> 01:51:29.300
How many of you can imagine running that for 98.6 miles?

3139
01:51:29.610 --> 01:51:31.587
Yeah. Harlem hill gets real big.

3140
01:51:31.587 --> 01:51:34.220
(all laughing)

3141
01:51:34.220 --> 01:51:37.540
I've had the privilege of watching Robbie

3142
01:51:37.540 --> 01:51:38.850
evolve as a runner.

3143
01:51:38.850 --> 01:51:42.481
I met him on the very first day of his attempt to cross

3144
01:51:42.481 --> 01:51:44.307
the country by foot.

3145
01:51:44.307 --> 01:51:46.040
It was like, who's this guy didn't really know

3146
01:51:46.040 --> 01:51:47.850
that much about him.

3147
01:51:47.850 --> 01:51:50.350
We ran for a couple of miles and then he went on his way

3148
01:51:50.350 --> 01:51:52.350
and I thought, I don't know if I'll ever see that guy again

3149
01:51:52.350 --> 01:51:53.908
or whether he's gonna make it or not.

3150
01:51:53.908 --> 01:51:54.741
(audience laughing)

3151
01:51:54.741 --> 01:51:58.540
And I've watched this individual continue to double down,

3152
01:51:58.540 --> 01:52:01.810
iterate and improve as an athlete.

3153
01:52:01.810 --> 01:52:05.270
And you've really matured into this incredible ambassador

3154
01:52:05.270 --> 01:52:06.103
of the sport.

3155
01:52:06.103 --> 01:52:08.098
So I just wanted to credit you for that.

3156
01:52:08.098 --> 01:52:12.710
And it's been so great to watch you take on and tackle

3157
01:52:12.710 --> 01:52:16.043
these challenges, I'm like catch a breath, you know,

3158
01:52:16.043 --> 01:52:18.190
take a break for a minute,

3159
01:52:18.190 --> 01:52:21.470
but let's talk about what you did this past summer,

3160
01:52:21.470 --> 01:52:22.470
the Colorado Crush.

3161
01:52:22.470 --> 01:52:25.260
So maybe explain to people what you spent

3162
01:52:25.260 --> 01:52:27.420
most of your entire summer doing.

3163
01:52:27.420 --> 01:52:29.620
Yeah. So the Colorado Crush consisted

3164
01:52:29.620 --> 01:52:30.710
of three parts.

3165
01:52:30.710 --> 01:52:32.550
It started with the Leadville trail series.

3166
01:52:32.550 --> 01:52:35.270
So Leadville town in Colorado at 10,000 feet,

3167
01:52:35.270 --> 01:52:37.810
it's the highest incorporated town in Colorado.

3168
01:52:37.810 --> 01:52:39.210
They have a rate series in June.

3169
01:52:39.210 --> 01:52:41.080
There is a marathon,

3170
01:52:41.080 --> 01:52:43.820
July, a 50 miler in August 100.

3171
01:52:43.820 --> 01:52:47.590
So I did all of those between the marathon and the 50 miler.

3172
01:52:47.590 --> 01:52:49.970
I ran the Colorado Trail.

3173
01:52:49.970 --> 01:52:52.840
So 485 miles, I did that in 11 days.

3174
01:52:52.840 --> 01:52:54.930
And then between the 50 miler and the 100,

3175
01:52:54.930 --> 01:52:59.470
I summited all 58 peaks over 14,000 feet in Colorado.

3176
01:52:59.470 --> 01:53:01.220
So cumulatively, when I got done,

3177
01:53:01.220 --> 01:53:03.130
it was just under 1200 miles.

3178
01:53:03.130 --> 01:53:06.850
And then the stat that I'm really proud of is it was 308,000

3179
01:53:06.850 --> 01:53:08.350
feet of elevation gain.

3180
01:53:08.350 --> 01:53:10.770
So that's the equivalent of summiting Everest

3181
01:53:10.770 --> 01:53:14.240
from sea to summit 10 and a half times in two months.

3182
01:53:14.240 --> 01:53:15.230
(audience laughing)

3183
01:53:15.230 --> 01:53:17.064
We are in the explorers club.

3184
01:53:17.064 --> 01:53:20.064
(audience laughing)

3185
01:53:21.488 --> 01:53:23.738
I'm like, I don't know where to go with that.

3186
01:53:24.850 --> 01:53:29.130
I think what's amazing is that it's easy for somebody

3187
01:53:29.130 --> 01:53:30.990
to hear that and think, well,

3188
01:53:30.990 --> 01:53:32.250
I can't even connect with that.

3189
01:53:32.250 --> 01:53:36.150
Like that's so outrageous that you must just be some freak

3190
01:53:36.150 --> 01:53:39.470
of nature, but you're not somebody who's been doing

3191
01:53:39.470 --> 01:53:41.480
this for actually all that long.

3192
01:53:41.480 --> 01:53:44.470
So maybe catch people up who don't know a little bit

3193
01:53:44.470 --> 01:53:46.630
about your backstory, how you got into running,

3194
01:53:46.630 --> 01:53:49.440
because this is relatively a recent phenomenon,

3195
01:53:49.440 --> 01:53:51.440
this kind of life overhaul that you've been on.

3196
01:53:51.440 --> 01:53:53.240
Yeah, I grew up in athletics.

3197
01:53:53.240 --> 01:53:55.240
I played team sports, predominantly football,

3198
01:53:55.240 --> 01:53:57.871
believe it or not, I was an offensive lineman

3199
01:53:57.871 --> 01:53:59.170
(all laughing)

3200
01:53:59.170 --> 01:54:01.158
How much did you weigh?

3201
01:54:01.158 --> 01:54:02.653
I was not like a big one.

3202
01:54:02.653 --> 01:54:05.530
I had fast feet and that kinda carried over,

3203
01:54:05.530 --> 01:54:07.010
but probably weighed about,

3204
01:54:07.010 --> 01:54:09.810
I think at my heaviest was actually after that in college,

3205
01:54:09.810 --> 01:54:13.143
I weighed like 225 from about 175 now,

3206
01:54:14.020 --> 01:54:17.290
and then post high school, a little bit of college.

3207
01:54:17.290 --> 01:54:20.360
I ended up in the restaurant industry for many years,

3208
01:54:20.360 --> 01:54:23.720
ending with owning being part owner

3209
01:54:23.720 --> 01:54:26.520
in some Neapolitan pizzerias in Austin, Texas.

3210
01:54:26.520 --> 01:54:29.550
And in that time I was just like living that restaurant

3211
01:54:29.550 --> 01:54:32.550
lifestyle work hard party real hard.

3212
01:54:32.550 --> 01:54:36.180
And it wasn't really adding up to me being the leader

3213
01:54:36.180 --> 01:54:38.160
I wanted to be for these restaurants

3214
01:54:38.160 --> 01:54:41.923
and on the encouragement of my fiance, Shelley,

3215
01:54:43.200 --> 01:54:46.320
she invited me on a two and a half mile run and that was it.

3216
01:54:46.320 --> 01:54:48.390
Like it changed my life.

3217
01:54:48.390 --> 01:54:49.570
And it's just been since then,

3218
01:54:49.570 --> 01:54:51.850
like two and a half miles turned into a half marathon,

3219
01:54:51.850 --> 01:54:55.990
two months later, a marathon the next year, 50 miler.

3220
01:54:55.990 --> 01:54:57.730
And then I left the restaurant and I didn't know

3221
01:54:57.730 --> 01:54:58.980
what I wanted to do next.

3222
01:54:58.980 --> 01:55:02.580
And some things kind of coral less.

3223
01:55:02.580 --> 01:55:04.650
And I decided I was going to run across

3224
01:55:04.650 --> 01:55:06.770
the United States and I would do it to create conversations

3225
01:55:06.770 --> 01:55:08.903
about better food choices and promote the efficacy

3226
01:55:08.903 --> 01:55:10.530
of a plant-based diet.

3227
01:55:10.530 --> 01:55:12.670
And so that was where this, like,

3228
01:55:12.670 --> 01:55:15.050
I kind of made my name in the running scene.

3229
01:55:15.050 --> 01:55:17.110
And then it's just been since then a couple of more big

3230
01:55:17.110 --> 01:55:20.720
efforts and refining what matters to me about running

3231
01:55:20.720 --> 01:55:23.860
and what are the things I need to be the best runner I can.

3232
01:55:23.860 --> 01:55:25.270
Mm-mmh.

3233
01:55:25.270 --> 01:55:28.380
So when you're, and this gets into kind of the theme

3234
01:55:28.380 --> 01:55:30.910
or the ethos of what today is all about,

3235
01:55:30.910 --> 01:55:33.870
like when you're running across America

3236
01:55:33.870 --> 01:55:36.800
or you're running Leadville or in the midst

3237
01:55:36.800 --> 01:55:40.210
of one of these unbelievable challenges,

3238
01:55:40.210 --> 01:55:42.450
like what is going on in your mind?

3239
01:55:42.450 --> 01:55:45.700
Like, what is your brain doing and how are you, you know,

3240
01:55:45.700 --> 01:55:48.400
staying on track and not letting you know,

3241
01:55:48.400 --> 01:55:50.733
kind of the dark demons get the best of you.

3242
01:55:51.590 --> 01:55:52.850
Yeah. I mean, for me,

3243
01:55:52.850 --> 01:55:55.270
like that's the problem with these things.

3244
01:55:55.270 --> 01:55:57.170
One leads to the next 'cause where I dream up,

3245
01:55:57.170 --> 01:55:59.720
the next challenge is usually on the one before it,

3246
01:56:00.560 --> 01:56:02.670
but while I'm out there, I mean, it's just,

3247
01:56:02.670 --> 01:56:06.820
running is where I go to better understand myself,

3248
01:56:06.820 --> 01:56:10.380
to just hash through all the parts of me that need work on.

3249
01:56:10.380 --> 01:56:12.480
And like, I think you can't really work on something

3250
01:56:12.480 --> 01:56:13.810
until you can identify it.

3251
01:56:13.810 --> 01:56:16.133
And on runs is where identify things.

3252
01:56:16.133 --> 01:56:17.970
It's like, okay, this is a part of me.

3253
01:56:17.970 --> 01:56:19.790
This is why I do this thing I do.

3254
01:56:19.790 --> 01:56:21.970
And now let me sift through what that is,

3255
01:56:21.970 --> 01:56:23.900
and what, what it means to me.

3256
01:56:23.900 --> 01:56:26.580
And like this summer I've been on this long journey

3257
01:56:26.580 --> 01:56:28.850
of my father died when I was really, really young.

3258
01:56:28.850 --> 01:56:30.853
I realized kind of through the Transcon run

3259
01:56:30.853 --> 01:56:33.790
that a lot of what I was doing was trying to define

3260
01:56:33.790 --> 01:56:35.630
what it means to be a man.

3261
01:56:35.630 --> 01:56:38.960
And then this summer starting with the Central Park

3262
01:56:38.960 --> 01:56:41.740
loop challenge, which I did here in New York,

3263
01:56:41.740 --> 01:56:44.180
right before that my uncle died, my dad's brother.

3264
01:56:44.180 --> 01:56:45.890
And that brought all that back to the surface.

3265
01:56:45.890 --> 01:56:47.750
And so from there it was sifting through this

3266
01:56:47.750 --> 01:56:50.074
of just like, what does this mean?

3267
01:56:50.074 --> 01:56:52.670
And I think what I came to through the Colorado Trail,

3268
01:56:52.670 --> 01:56:54.340
especially part of the summers effort

3269
01:56:54.340 --> 01:56:56.990
is I came to peace with that and I realized

3270
01:56:56.990 --> 01:56:59.020
that I'm no longer looking for what it means.

3271
01:56:59.020 --> 01:57:00.700
I'm just expressing it.

3272
01:57:00.700 --> 01:57:03.200
I'm expressing my version of what it means to be a man.

3273
01:57:03.200 --> 01:57:06.280
So that was like the big thing that came out of this summer,

3274
01:57:06.280 --> 01:57:08.800
but it's always something new and it's always a place for me

3275
01:57:08.800 --> 01:57:10.820
to yeah, just figure those things out

3276
01:57:10.820 --> 01:57:12.350
and then dream about the next thing.

3277
01:57:12.350 --> 01:57:17.060
Yeah. I feel like there's a fine line between

3278
01:57:17.060 --> 01:57:21.040
the indulgence of running at the ultra level,

3279
01:57:21.040 --> 01:57:26.040
like the way that you engage with it on the one hand,

3280
01:57:26.150 --> 01:57:30.770
it can be lever for ordinary self improvement,

3281
01:57:30.770 --> 01:57:33.880
self understanding, (indistinct chattering)

3282
01:57:33.880 --> 01:57:37.660
for unlocking hidden potential coming the best version

3283
01:57:37.660 --> 01:57:38.660
of yourself.

3284
01:57:38.660 --> 01:57:43.440
But the flip side, it can literally use running to run away

3285
01:57:43.440 --> 01:57:46.930
from those very things, those demons that you're trying

3286
01:57:46.930 --> 01:57:47.763
to hide from.

3287
01:57:47.763 --> 01:57:50.010
And I see both versions of that.

3288
01:57:50.010 --> 01:57:52.540
So I'm curious whether you've come up against that.

3289
01:57:52.540 --> 01:57:55.900
Like at some point, if you're doing so many challenges,

3290
01:57:55.900 --> 01:57:57.447
the question has to be asked like,

3291
01:57:57.447 --> 01:58:00.130
are you running away from something or are you running

3292
01:58:00.130 --> 01:58:00.963
towards something?

3293
01:58:00.963 --> 01:58:03.190
So how do you kind of think about that?

3294
01:58:03.190 --> 01:58:04.745
And get on with that?

3295
01:58:04.745 --> 01:58:07.190
I mean, thus far, just all the markers say

3296
01:58:07.190 --> 01:58:08.540
that it's going in the right direction,

3297
01:58:08.540 --> 01:58:10.009
I'm running towards something.

3298
01:58:10.009 --> 01:58:11.420
You're in a healthy relationship still.

3299
01:58:11.420 --> 01:58:15.610
Yeah. I've still got my fiance and yeah.

3300
01:58:15.610 --> 01:58:18.520
I mean, thus far it's, it's redefining me as a person

3301
01:58:18.520 --> 01:58:21.090
in a way that seems all positive.

3302
01:58:21.090 --> 01:58:23.270
I understand that there's that other side of it,

3303
01:58:23.270 --> 01:58:24.520
my mom will ask me that one.

3304
01:58:24.520 --> 01:58:26.050
She's like, "Is this just an addiction?"

3305
01:58:26.050 --> 01:58:27.480
And I was like, "It's better than the old ones."

3306
01:58:27.480 --> 01:58:30.290
Like, you know,
(audience laughing)

3307
01:58:30.290 --> 01:58:33.613
Yeah. My friend Mishika said to me.

3308
01:58:34.470 --> 01:58:37.120
'Cause he gets asked that same question,

3309
01:58:37.120 --> 01:58:40.740
the way he answers it is, "the drink or the drug was always

3310
01:58:40.740 --> 01:58:42.920
the easy choice putting on the running shoes

3311
01:58:42.920 --> 01:58:44.240
is the hard choice."

3312
01:58:44.240 --> 01:58:45.073
Absolutely.

3313
01:58:45.073 --> 01:58:46.510
And it builds resilience in the rest of your life.

3314
01:58:46.510 --> 01:58:48.170
You know, like just the getting out on the run.

3315
01:58:48.170 --> 01:58:49.450
I live in Denver, Colorado.

3316
01:58:49.450 --> 01:58:51.040
And like, as I was preparing

3317
01:58:51.040 --> 01:58:52.530
for the Central Park loop challenge,

3318
01:58:52.530 --> 01:58:55.037
like a big thing I was doing was getting out

3319
01:58:55.037 --> 01:58:56.810
and running when it was really cold.

3320
01:58:56.810 --> 01:58:58.900
And a lot of that was just about gaining that,

3321
01:58:58.900 --> 01:59:01.470
get up and go muscle, you know, just like really,

3322
01:59:01.470 --> 01:59:03.020
'cause I'm gonna do a loop around Central Park.

3323
01:59:03.020 --> 01:59:05.840
I'm gonna stop. I'm gonna see my, my family, my friends,

3324
01:59:05.840 --> 01:59:07.040
I got to get back out there.

3325
01:59:07.040 --> 01:59:09.640
And so just building that resiliency.

3326
01:59:09.640 --> 01:59:10.650
Yeah. So thus far for me,

3327
01:59:10.650 --> 01:59:12.440
I feel like running has been very positive.

3328
01:59:12.440 --> 01:59:16.930
Yeah. So what does Free Association Run

3329
01:59:16.930 --> 01:59:17.930
mean to you?

3330
01:59:17.930 --> 01:59:19.205
Robbie Balenger?

3331
01:59:19.205 --> 01:59:22.070
I think like, to me, like when I think of that,

3332
01:59:22.070 --> 01:59:25.660
I think a lot about Knox Robinson's here, right?

3333
01:59:25.660 --> 01:59:27.130
And he's mentioned,

3334
01:59:27.130 --> 01:59:29.750
I think it was him that said like, "Running his rebellion."

3335
01:59:29.750 --> 01:59:31.750
I think running is also selfish,

3336
01:59:31.750 --> 01:59:33.800
but I don't think selfish has to be a bad word.

3337
01:59:33.800 --> 01:59:36.570
Like it's a place where you get to commune with yourself.

3338
01:59:36.570 --> 01:59:39.570
And this summer, what I realized being in the mountains

3339
01:59:39.570 --> 01:59:43.550
compared to running on the side of the road is like,

3340
01:59:43.550 --> 01:59:46.330
there's this world that we live in and all the technology

3341
01:59:46.330 --> 01:59:48.050
and everything, it has all these great things,

3342
01:59:48.050 --> 01:59:49.710
but it also can really beat you down.

3343
01:59:49.710 --> 01:59:50.970
And like when I got out on the trail,

3344
01:59:50.970 --> 01:59:53.250
I realized none of that was there even to more of an extent

3345
01:59:53.250 --> 01:59:54.410
than I normally do.

3346
01:59:54.410 --> 01:59:56.440
But in that, that running's kind of selfish.

3347
01:59:56.440 --> 01:59:59.730
We just to get to disassociate with all of these things

3348
01:59:59.730 --> 02:00:02.260
that just can kind of drive us nuts in a way,

3349
02:00:02.260 --> 02:00:04.620
even when we don't know it, and you get to be centered

3350
02:00:04.620 --> 02:00:07.343
with yourself and you get to learn from yourself,

3351
02:00:08.210 --> 02:00:09.740
the most growth comes internally.

3352
02:00:09.740 --> 02:00:11.720
And again, it's back to that idea of just sifting through

3353
02:00:11.720 --> 02:00:14.080
who I am, the things I wanna do.

3354
02:00:14.080 --> 02:00:16.150
And often you, because you're running,

3355
02:00:16.150 --> 02:00:18.170
there's no barriers to where your thought goes.

3356
02:00:18.170 --> 02:00:19.630
Like often I come home with an idea

3357
02:00:19.630 --> 02:00:20.670
and I have to reign it in.

3358
02:00:20.670 --> 02:00:22.070
I'm like, that was great.

3359
02:00:22.070 --> 02:00:23.522
But like, let's be realistic here.

3360
02:00:23.522 --> 02:00:26.430
(Robbie laughing)

3361
02:00:26.430 --> 02:00:27.670
One of the problems that I have

3362
02:00:27.670 --> 02:00:29.640
is I'll have those sparks of inspiration

3363
02:00:29.640 --> 02:00:32.010
when I'm out running and I don't wanna stop

3364
02:00:32.010 --> 02:00:35.060
and like type them into a note or like record a voice, like,

3365
02:00:35.060 --> 02:00:36.940
is that interrupts the thing?

3366
02:00:36.940 --> 02:00:41.020
But if I don't do that, then they become very elusive

3367
02:00:41.020 --> 02:00:43.680
and they might like I'll forget them quickly.

3368
02:00:43.680 --> 02:00:46.890
So it's always a challenge to figure out how to like capture

3369
02:00:46.890 --> 02:00:49.830
the best of what might percolate up during a run

3370
02:00:49.830 --> 02:00:52.420
versus interrupting that flow.

3371
02:00:52.420 --> 02:00:53.643
How do you deal with that?

3372
02:00:53.643 --> 02:00:55.150
I think I wait until it happens

3373
02:00:55.150 --> 02:00:57.660
a couple of times, you know, I run a lot.

3374
02:00:57.660 --> 02:00:59.960
So it's like when those ideas come and pop into my head

3375
02:00:59.960 --> 02:01:01.610
a couple of times on a run and then they resonate

3376
02:01:01.610 --> 02:01:03.750
and I remember them, I'm sure there's a lot of missed,

3377
02:01:03.750 --> 02:01:06.030
but also sometimes stop and write them down.

3378
02:01:06.030 --> 02:01:08.450
Like I'm not a very fast runner.

3379
02:01:08.450 --> 02:01:09.790
I just go really far.

3380
02:01:09.790 --> 02:01:12.670
And in that, like running should be enjoyable.

3381
02:01:12.670 --> 02:01:13.520
So when I'm out on a run,

3382
02:01:13.520 --> 02:01:15.090
I'm not usually worried about splits.

3383
02:01:15.090 --> 02:01:18.310
I'll take that time to just like calm down,

3384
02:01:18.310 --> 02:01:19.890
write it down and keep going.

3385
02:01:19.890 --> 02:01:21.135
But I wouldn't say often more.

3386
02:01:21.135 --> 02:01:23.920
It's just those things that come up often.

3387
02:01:23.920 --> 02:01:25.680
I mean, they need to be paid attention to.

3388
02:01:25.680 --> 02:01:27.610
Right. If you follow Robbie on Instagram,

3389
02:01:27.610 --> 02:01:30.640
he always shares photos that he takes during his run.

3390
02:01:30.640 --> 02:01:32.930
So I know that you're at least stopping a little bit

3391
02:01:32.930 --> 02:01:34.933
to like enjoy the experience.

3392
02:01:36.040 --> 02:01:39.900
And I was joking after the run that we just had,

3393
02:01:39.900 --> 02:01:42.990
the intention for the run was for it to be silent for us

3394
02:01:42.990 --> 02:01:46.110
to have that free association experience,

3395
02:01:46.110 --> 02:01:49.590
to kind of pay attention to the breath, to, you know,

3396
02:01:49.590 --> 02:01:54.080
get into that higher state of consciousness where creativity

3397
02:01:54.080 --> 02:01:57.910
can percolate up and we can associate disparate ideas

3398
02:01:57.910 --> 02:02:00.110
and kind of have that spark of inspiration.

3399
02:02:00.110 --> 02:02:03.120
But instead there was a lot of talk nonetheless,

3400
02:02:03.120 --> 02:02:05.660
and I was as guilty of that as anyone else.

3401
02:02:05.660 --> 02:02:08.170
And it made me think like, you know, the idea was like,

3402
02:02:08.170 --> 02:02:11.780
come back and share what that experience was about

3403
02:02:11.780 --> 02:02:13.120
or what came up for you.

3404
02:02:13.120 --> 02:02:15.395
And for me it was a couple of things.

3405
02:02:15.395 --> 02:02:18.700
One like extreme gratitude to just be in New York City

3406
02:02:18.700 --> 02:02:21.840
in the fall and run like what an extraordinary privilege

3407
02:02:21.840 --> 02:02:24.040
it is to run a New York City

3408
02:02:24.040 --> 02:02:26.700
and to be part of this community.

3409
02:02:26.700 --> 02:02:28.650
Like I do a lot of my running alone.

3410
02:02:28.650 --> 02:02:30.630
I live in a pretty remote place

3411
02:02:30.630 --> 02:02:32.860
and I'm just out trail running by myself.

3412
02:02:32.860 --> 02:02:35.420
And it reminded me how important community is,

3413
02:02:35.420 --> 02:02:38.410
and how good that felt to be with a group

3414
02:02:38.410 --> 02:02:39.950
of like-minded people,

3415
02:02:39.950 --> 02:02:42.230
and running for me, and my relationship with it is,

3416
02:02:42.230 --> 02:02:46.420
is this combination of that's so low experience

3417
02:02:46.420 --> 02:02:48.040
where you can go deep on yourself

3418
02:02:48.040 --> 02:02:52.130
and really kind of be in that active meditation space

3419
02:02:52.130 --> 02:02:57.130
that is so healthy and kind of healing for me.

3420
02:02:58.000 --> 02:02:59.770
And then on the flip side of that,

3421
02:02:59.770 --> 02:03:02.020
to have the community experience and you,

3422
02:03:02.020 --> 02:03:04.190
as somebody who goes really long, I mean,

3423
02:03:04.190 --> 02:03:06.404
I wanna talk about the Colorado Crush and that experience

3424
02:03:06.404 --> 02:03:07.900
in a little bit more detail.

3425
02:03:07.900 --> 02:03:10.340
But one of the things that I think was notable

3426
02:03:10.340 --> 02:03:14.430
about it was this combination of these solo expeditions,

3427
02:03:14.430 --> 02:03:16.130
but then you would pop in on these races

3428
02:03:16.130 --> 02:03:19.780
and have kind of that communal like suck or, you know,

3429
02:03:19.780 --> 02:03:24.550
to get fed by, you know, your fellow community runners.

3430
02:03:24.550 --> 02:03:25.383
Yeah, absolutely.

3431
02:03:25.383 --> 02:03:26.990
It was a place to come in and commune with others.

3432
02:03:26.990 --> 02:03:29.870
Absolutely. Like the effort of the Colorado Crush

3433
02:03:29.870 --> 02:03:30.810
was those three efforts.

3434
02:03:30.810 --> 02:03:33.050
And they all kind of ended up being meaning something

3435
02:03:33.050 --> 02:03:35.150
different for me individually.

3436
02:03:35.150 --> 02:03:38.010
And it was that like the races were communal,

3437
02:03:38.010 --> 02:03:39.500
it was a place to celebrate running

3438
02:03:39.500 --> 02:03:41.360
with all these other people in this beautiful town

3439
02:03:41.360 --> 02:03:42.193
of Leadville.

3440
02:03:43.060 --> 02:03:46.890
And then the Colorado Trail was very introspective.

3441
02:03:46.890 --> 02:03:50.170
It was just me on the trail, long swaths of time

3442
02:03:50.170 --> 02:03:52.280
while I was by myself, like there was one day I was out,

3443
02:03:52.280 --> 02:03:55.160
it was 56 miles and it took me 19 and a half hours.

3444
02:03:55.160 --> 02:03:56.920
And I didn't have any crew through all of that.

3445
02:03:56.920 --> 02:04:00.480
So like a lot of time to just commune with myself.

3446
02:04:00.480 --> 02:04:03.720
And then the fourteeners were just this immense amount

3447
02:04:03.720 --> 02:04:04.580
of gratitude.

3448
02:04:04.580 --> 02:04:07.020
Like when you get to wake up every day and see sunrise

3449
02:04:07.020 --> 02:04:09.250
on the top of a mountain, like, you're just like,

3450
02:04:09.250 --> 02:04:10.310
I'm so lucky.

3451
02:04:10.310 --> 02:04:12.660
Like it was just so much gratitude for it.

3452
02:04:12.660 --> 02:04:15.210
So each of them kind of had their own specific meaning

3453
02:04:15.210 --> 02:04:16.170
in there.

3454
02:04:16.170 --> 02:04:18.060
So you were driving around in this van, right?

3455
02:04:18.060 --> 02:04:18.893
It had your name on it.

3456
02:04:18.893 --> 02:04:20.190
Do you still have the van?

3457
02:04:20.190 --> 02:04:21.023
Absolutely.

3458
02:04:21.023 --> 02:04:22.170
Did you drive the van here?

3459
02:04:22.170 --> 02:04:23.402
No, no, no, no.

3460
02:04:23.402 --> 02:04:25.324
I was hoping you'd have, when I showed up here,

3461
02:04:25.324 --> 02:04:26.157
it would be parked up front.

3462
02:04:26.157 --> 02:04:26.990
Yeah, right.

3463
02:04:26.990 --> 02:04:28.410
No, I drove enough this summer,

3464
02:04:28.410 --> 02:04:31.530
Colorado, you can drive a lot in that state if you want to,

3465
02:04:31.530 --> 02:04:33.380
like, I've seen every inch of it, so.

3466
02:04:34.370 --> 02:04:35.240
Let's talk a little bit

3467
02:04:35.240 --> 02:04:36.500
about the run across America.

3468
02:04:36.500 --> 02:04:39.530
I mean, we did a on that, where we went into it at length,

3469
02:04:39.530 --> 02:04:41.870
but a lot of people here may not have heard that.

3470
02:04:41.870 --> 02:04:43.630
Maybe just share a few.

3471
02:04:43.630 --> 02:04:44.463
Well, first of all,

3472
02:04:44.463 --> 02:04:48.300
explain the process of how you did that.

3473
02:04:48.300 --> 02:04:50.250
How many miles you were running every day

3474
02:04:50.250 --> 02:04:53.600
and then maybe share a couple highlights and low points

3475
02:04:53.600 --> 02:04:55.840
and, you know, tackling that challenge.

3476
02:04:55.840 --> 02:04:58.620
Yeah. So it was 3,175 miles.

3477
02:04:58.620 --> 02:04:59.930
I did it in 75 days.

3478
02:04:59.930 --> 02:05:02.200
So from Huntington Beach, just south of Los Angeles

3479
02:05:02.200 --> 02:05:06.670
to Central Park, it was me really venturing

3480
02:05:06.670 --> 02:05:10.790
into this big ultra endurance efforts and looking back,

3481
02:05:10.790 --> 02:05:13.880
like I probably bit off a tiny bit more than I could chew.

3482
02:05:13.880 --> 02:05:17.180
I got it done, but I pushed my mind,

3483
02:05:17.180 --> 02:05:19.310
my body everyday to the limit.

3484
02:05:19.310 --> 02:05:22.580
And it was done to again about being plant-based

3485
02:05:22.580 --> 02:05:23.680
and that was new to me.

3486
02:05:23.680 --> 02:05:27.150
So it was something I really wanted to talk to others about.

3487
02:05:27.150 --> 02:05:28.590
And I wanted to prove to myself

3488
02:05:28.590 --> 02:05:30.950
that it was the right choice.

3489
02:05:30.950 --> 02:05:32.880
And I proved to myself that it was,

3490
02:05:32.880 --> 02:05:35.350
it's definitely what works for me.

3491
02:05:35.350 --> 02:05:38.650
Along the way, there were lots of highs and lows

3492
02:05:38.650 --> 02:05:41.040
the first month, much harder than the rest of it.

3493
02:05:41.040 --> 02:05:42.620
Well, all of it was really hard,

3494
02:05:42.620 --> 02:05:45.650
but it got a little bit easier after a month,

3495
02:05:45.650 --> 02:05:48.990
day 19, I really probably came to my darkest place

3496
02:05:48.990 --> 02:05:50.860
and ended up having to take a day off.

3497
02:05:50.860 --> 02:05:53.130
I had like really bad tendonitis in my leg.

3498
02:05:53.130 --> 02:05:55.700
And in that day, though, in retrospect,

3499
02:05:55.700 --> 02:05:57.892
a lot of growth came out of it.

3500
02:05:57.892 --> 02:05:59.910
It was the one moment where I wasn't sure

3501
02:05:59.910 --> 02:06:01.600
if I'd be able to continue.

3502
02:06:01.600 --> 02:06:05.580
And in that you kind of give yourself up to do the energy

3503
02:06:05.580 --> 02:06:07.980
of the world, a higher power, whatever it might be.

3504
02:06:07.980 --> 02:06:09.630
And that was really important for me.

3505
02:06:09.630 --> 02:06:12.710
Like somebody that just grips tightly to control,

3506
02:06:12.710 --> 02:06:16.040
letting that go and realizing that it was too big for me

3507
02:06:16.040 --> 02:06:17.900
to just like force it the whole time

3508
02:06:17.900 --> 02:06:19.170
and having to go with the flow,

3509
02:06:19.170 --> 02:06:20.830
put one foot in front of the other

3510
02:06:20.830 --> 02:06:22.610
and yeah, take it day by day.

3511
02:06:22.610 --> 02:06:24.320
So that was definitely like low,

3512
02:06:24.320 --> 02:06:26.900
but also one of the highest points.

3513
02:06:26.900 --> 02:06:28.300
The Navajo Nation in general

3514
02:06:28.300 --> 02:06:29.730
is just a really beautiful place.

3515
02:06:29.730 --> 02:06:32.660
I ran through that for like eight days,

3516
02:06:32.660 --> 02:06:35.830
a special, special place that I truly loved.

3517
02:06:35.830 --> 02:06:37.010
Yeah. It was just great.

3518
02:06:37.010 --> 02:06:39.222
Appalachians will absolutely crush your soul though.

3519
02:06:39.222 --> 02:06:42.543
Like Pennsylvania really hard, like, yeah.

3520
02:06:42.543 --> 02:06:43.530
(audience laughing)

3521
02:06:43.530 --> 02:06:46.110
He's so casual and cavalier about this stuff.

3522
02:06:46.110 --> 02:06:47.210
Like it's no big deal.

3523
02:06:47.210 --> 02:06:49.460
Like it's crazy what you've done.

3524
02:06:49.460 --> 02:06:54.290
The thing that really stands out for me about you

3525
02:06:54.290 --> 02:06:56.210
and kind of how you approach your running

3526
02:06:56.210 --> 02:07:00.880
and your athleticism is that there's this service piece

3527
02:07:00.880 --> 02:07:03.690
to it in that when you're not in the midst of tackling

3528
02:07:03.690 --> 02:07:07.110
your own challenge, you pop in on these other people's

3529
02:07:07.110 --> 02:07:08.700
challenges and help them along.

3530
02:07:08.700 --> 02:07:12.740
So you were a big part of Hellah Sidibe's

3531
02:07:12.740 --> 02:07:15.320
run across America, the other summer he also ended up

3532
02:07:15.320 --> 02:07:18.620
here in New York City and would've been here today,

3533
02:07:18.620 --> 02:07:20.920
but he's out of town, I guess.

3534
02:07:20.920 --> 02:07:23.370
And then also you were just in the UK helping out

3535
02:07:23.370 --> 02:07:25.880
some other guys, on some other crazy challenge.

3536
02:07:25.880 --> 02:07:29.940
And I just like the idea that you give yourself over,

3537
02:07:29.940 --> 02:07:31.740
like, there's a humility to that.

3538
02:07:31.740 --> 02:07:35.310
I think that you have this practice of showing up in service

3539
02:07:35.310 --> 02:07:38.240
to other people who are trying to do things similar

3540
02:07:38.240 --> 02:07:39.350
to what you've done.

3541
02:07:39.350 --> 02:07:43.080
So talk a little bit about how that plays into how you think

3542
02:07:43.080 --> 02:07:45.510
of yourself as an athlete and a man,

3543
02:07:45.510 --> 02:07:48.830
because this whole idea of like how to be a man

3544
02:07:48.830 --> 02:07:50.510
is a big part for you.

3545
02:07:50.510 --> 02:07:51.940
Yeah. With both of those guys, like,

3546
02:07:51.940 --> 02:07:56.210
I think the, like why we're the three of us,

3547
02:07:56.210 --> 02:07:58.400
I'm dear friends with both of them now,

3548
02:07:58.400 --> 02:08:01.960
but it all came from a place of meeting them both in,

3549
02:08:01.960 --> 02:08:02.793
through our meeting,

3550
02:08:02.793 --> 02:08:05.000
understanding that they wanted their challenge

3551
02:08:05.000 --> 02:08:06.660
as bad as I wanted mine.

3552
02:08:06.660 --> 02:08:09.610
And you need people to help you along the way.

3553
02:08:09.610 --> 02:08:12.460
And you know, in mind, like certain people stood up

3554
02:08:12.460 --> 02:08:15.320
and took the lead to help me make it happen.

3555
02:08:15.320 --> 02:08:18.190
And I saw an opportunity to do that for both of these guys.

3556
02:08:18.190 --> 02:08:20.110
And they're just two people too that are just like,

3557
02:08:20.110 --> 02:08:21.450
you know, there's those people in life

3558
02:08:21.450 --> 02:08:22.460
that just resonate with you.

3559
02:08:22.460 --> 02:08:23.480
And like, they're like brothers

3560
02:08:23.480 --> 02:08:24.780
and you just know it right away.

3561
02:08:24.780 --> 02:08:26.973
And luckily both of them are that for me.

3562
02:08:27.920 --> 02:08:30.460
And then, you know, they're both very different people.

3563
02:08:30.460 --> 02:08:32.940
I mean, Hellah, if anyone is familiar with Hellah,

3564
02:08:32.940 --> 02:08:34.330
I think most people are at this point,

3565
02:08:34.330 --> 02:08:35.543
he's becoming an icon.

3566
02:08:37.040 --> 02:08:38.950
You just are attracted to him.

3567
02:08:38.950 --> 02:08:41.350
He's just, he's just such a positive person.

3568
02:08:41.350 --> 02:08:43.027
And I try to be positive,

3569
02:08:43.027 --> 02:08:46.030
but the more around Hellah it rubs off.

3570
02:08:46.030 --> 02:08:47.440
So I get something out of it too.

3571
02:08:47.440 --> 02:08:48.960
And that's a really big deal.

3572
02:08:48.960 --> 02:08:50.810
And then William Goodge, who I just helped,

3573
02:08:50.810 --> 02:08:52.247
he did a 48 and 30.

3574
02:08:52.247 --> 02:08:56.460
He ran 48 marathons in each county in England, in 30 days,

3575
02:08:56.460 --> 02:08:59.010
ending with the London marathon where he ran a 306.

3576
02:09:00.867 --> 02:09:03.250
48 marathons in 30 days.

3577
02:09:03.250 --> 02:09:05.110
Yeah, yeah.

3578
02:09:05.110 --> 02:09:06.440
In different places?

3579
02:09:06.440 --> 02:09:07.273
Each county.

3580
02:09:07.273 --> 02:09:08.517
So we were hopping around.

3581
02:09:08.517 --> 02:09:10.987
And with him, it was a little bit different.

3582
02:09:10.987 --> 02:09:15.540
When I met him, I went over right after we did our podcasts,

3583
02:09:15.540 --> 02:09:19.310
I went over to the UK and he did joggle, John O'Groats

3584
02:09:19.310 --> 02:09:20.143
to Land Ends.

3585
02:09:20.143 --> 02:09:21.350
And he started at the tip of Scotland

3586
02:09:21.350 --> 02:09:22.630
and went to the bottom.

3587
02:09:22.630 --> 02:09:23.590
He was not ready.

3588
02:09:23.590 --> 02:09:24.830
He bluffed his training.

3589
02:09:24.830 --> 02:09:27.187
16 days, he got it done.

3590
02:09:27.187 --> 02:09:28.770
It was like 59 miles a day.

3591
02:09:28.770 --> 02:09:31.610
But he was on the heels of his mother passing, Mandy.

3592
02:09:31.610 --> 02:09:33.310
She died of cancer.

3593
02:09:33.310 --> 02:09:36.913
And so like our bond comes through that loss.

3594
02:09:37.905 --> 02:09:39.127
Yeah. 'Cause my father died when I was young.

3595
02:09:39.127 --> 02:09:41.150
That's a lot to do with why I run.

3596
02:09:41.150 --> 02:09:42.950
William definitely runs for Mandy,

3597
02:09:42.950 --> 02:09:45.570
and I don't know anybody else in my circle

3598
02:09:45.570 --> 02:09:46.550
that that's why we run.

3599
02:09:46.550 --> 02:09:48.772
So that's kind of our kinsmanship and he's 10 years younger

3600
02:09:48.772 --> 02:09:51.170
than me, and he's like a little brother,

3601
02:09:51.170 --> 02:09:53.930
like a bigger little brother, but yeah.

3602
02:09:53.930 --> 02:09:55.693
He's yeah, so those are my.

3603
02:09:57.510 --> 02:10:00.280
When you were back in the restaurant industry,

3604
02:10:00.280 --> 02:10:04.850
could you have imagined this life that you're living now?

3605
02:10:04.850 --> 02:10:05.750
Absolutely not.

3606
02:10:05.750 --> 02:10:07.400
It wasn't that long ago.

3607
02:10:08.416 --> 02:10:09.730
No, it still shocks me.

3608
02:10:09.730 --> 02:10:11.060
My birthday was just the other day.

3609
02:10:11.060 --> 02:10:13.010
And I posted on Instagram about that.

3610
02:10:13.010 --> 02:10:14.280
I was like five years ago.

3611
02:10:14.280 --> 02:10:16.110
I never thought I am where I am now.

3612
02:10:16.110 --> 02:10:18.560
And what I realized about that that's really exciting

3613
02:10:18.560 --> 02:10:19.810
is that means, I don't know where I'm gonna be

3614
02:10:19.810 --> 02:10:20.860
in five more years.

3615
02:10:20.860 --> 02:10:23.910
And that's really fun to like, know that every day is new.

3616
02:10:23.910 --> 02:10:26.580
And if you embrace life and go with the flow and sometimes

3617
02:10:26.580 --> 02:10:29.800
force things like life can be really exciting.

3618
02:10:29.800 --> 02:10:33.220
Yeah. So there may be people in this room

3619
02:10:33.220 --> 02:10:36.730
who are in the earlier stages of trying to figure out

3620
02:10:36.730 --> 02:10:39.130
who they wanna be, the trajectory that they're on.

3621
02:10:39.130 --> 02:10:43.320
Maybe they're considering some life changes or some pivots.

3622
02:10:43.320 --> 02:10:46.030
So talk a little bit about how you figured that out

3623
02:10:46.030 --> 02:10:48.210
for yourself and how you got yourself into a state

3624
02:10:48.210 --> 02:10:52.510
of mind to make some pretty drastic changes

3625
02:10:52.510 --> 02:10:56.330
and incur some risk and kind of blaze this unique path

3626
02:10:56.330 --> 02:10:57.800
that you've been on.

3627
02:10:57.800 --> 02:11:00.610
Yeah. I mean, everything I do,

3628
02:11:00.610 --> 02:11:04.710
I put myself into wholeheartedly like 110% everything.

3629
02:11:04.710 --> 02:11:08.520
And with that, like you give yourself fully,

3630
02:11:08.520 --> 02:11:10.320
there's a point, there might be a breaking point

3631
02:11:10.320 --> 02:11:13.180
where you've given yourself so much that you kind of break.

3632
02:11:13.180 --> 02:11:14.013
And that's kind of what happened with me

3633
02:11:14.013 --> 02:11:14.970
with the restaurant.

3634
02:11:14.970 --> 02:11:17.210
It was almost like there wasn't a choice.

3635
02:11:17.210 --> 02:11:18.043
Like it was over.

3636
02:11:18.043 --> 02:11:19.510
I had to take a break from that.

3637
02:11:19.510 --> 02:11:24.510
And with that, my mom always has told me when I was a kid,

3638
02:11:24.727 --> 02:11:26.380
"You can do anything you set your mind to."

3639
02:11:26.380 --> 02:11:28.660
And so like getting into, running to this degree,

3640
02:11:28.660 --> 02:11:31.790
running across the United States was kind of like,

3641
02:11:31.790 --> 02:11:33.440
all right, let me see if that's true.

3642
02:11:33.440 --> 02:11:34.610
And, and I did it.

3643
02:11:34.610 --> 02:11:38.590
So I like, my thought like,

3644
02:11:38.590 --> 02:11:40.240
I've come to this recently is like,

3645
02:11:40.240 --> 02:11:44.660
life's gonna throw you shit, no matter what you do,

3646
02:11:44.660 --> 02:11:45.900
you're gonna be disappointed.

3647
02:11:45.900 --> 02:11:48.010
There's gonna be things obstacles to overcome.

3648
02:11:48.010 --> 02:11:50.550
Whether you sit on the couch or you run across the country,

3649
02:11:50.550 --> 02:11:52.450
so you might as well do what you wanna do.

3650
02:11:52.450 --> 02:11:55.590
Like get out there, put yourself out there

3651
02:11:55.590 --> 02:11:58.390
and make that your obstacles.

3652
02:11:58.390 --> 02:12:00.180
And then at the end, you'll be where you wanna be

3653
02:12:00.180 --> 02:12:02.080
instead of still sitting on the couch.

3654
02:12:03.390 --> 02:12:06.460
But yeah, and that's just keep redefining myself

3655
02:12:06.460 --> 02:12:08.330
and try new things for the sake of,

3656
02:12:08.330 --> 02:12:09.580
we only have one life to live.

3657
02:12:09.580 --> 02:12:10.670
Maybe, maybe there's more,

3658
02:12:10.670 --> 02:12:12.580
but that's all we know about for sure.

3659
02:12:12.580 --> 02:12:14.610
And I think that comes from losing my father

3660
02:12:14.610 --> 02:12:15.800
at such a young age.

3661
02:12:15.800 --> 02:12:19.330
Like I just wanna embrace every day and make the best

3662
02:12:19.330 --> 02:12:21.410
of it because you don't know when it's over.

3663
02:12:21.410 --> 02:12:23.740
And that, I think that makes me never be able to like,

3664
02:12:23.740 --> 02:12:27.390
ideally sit by and not do what really speaks to me.

3665
02:12:27.390 --> 02:12:28.320
I mean, I have my lows.

3666
02:12:28.320 --> 02:12:31.270
I've even since running across the U.S.

3667
02:12:31.270 --> 02:12:33.340
it hasn't been a direct path to me sitting here with you

3668
02:12:33.340 --> 02:12:34.510
right now.

3669
02:12:34.510 --> 02:12:37.870
I, at one point, kinda didn't know if I could do it.

3670
02:12:37.870 --> 02:12:40.220
I lost like the confidence and I tried something else.

3671
02:12:40.220 --> 02:12:42.310
And then it was like, that definitely doesn't work.

3672
02:12:42.310 --> 02:12:43.370
Let me get back into this.

3673
02:12:43.370 --> 02:12:45.930
So nothing's linear and perfect.

3674
02:12:45.930 --> 02:12:47.470
Even as I sit here.

3675
02:12:47.470 --> 02:12:48.830
On that point specifically,

3676
02:12:48.830 --> 02:12:50.880
talk a little bit about failure

3677
02:12:50.880 --> 02:12:52.300
and how you think about failure.

3678
02:12:52.300 --> 02:12:55.070
I mean, obviously you can't do anything extraordinary

3679
02:12:55.070 --> 02:12:58.210
without incurring the risk of failure and failing time

3680
02:12:58.210 --> 02:12:59.043
and time again.

3681
02:12:59.043 --> 02:13:01.510
So how do you conceptualize that?

3682
02:13:01.510 --> 02:13:03.143
To me, it's just like,

3683
02:13:04.110 --> 02:13:07.610
it's all in mindset and we get to choose what we hang on to.

3684
02:13:07.610 --> 02:13:11.780
And like, I just choose to celebrate my victories

3685
02:13:11.780 --> 02:13:15.160
and take my defeats and throw them to the wind.

3686
02:13:15.160 --> 02:13:16.363
Like, what's the point?

3687
02:13:18.510 --> 02:13:21.330
We can be constructive if we have the right mindset

3688
02:13:21.330 --> 02:13:23.910
and to me, failures are going to happen,

3689
02:13:23.910 --> 02:13:26.490
but they're just part of the learning process.

3690
02:13:26.490 --> 02:13:28.580
And you're gonna learn something from it,

3691
02:13:28.580 --> 02:13:30.880
but you get to decide if you're gonna let it beat you,

3692
02:13:30.880 --> 02:13:33.570
or you're just gonna not like beat it, just let it go,

3693
02:13:33.570 --> 02:13:36.040
like, get it out of the way and keep going.

3694
02:13:36.040 --> 02:13:38.320
I would suspect there's some people

3695
02:13:38.320 --> 02:13:41.990
here who maybe have a dream or a big idea

3696
02:13:41.990 --> 02:13:44.420
that have something that they want to accomplish

3697
02:13:44.420 --> 02:13:45.253
in their life.

3698
02:13:45.253 --> 02:13:46.720
And maybe that's an idea that recurs,

3699
02:13:46.720 --> 02:13:48.900
when they go out and run and they kind of meditate

3700
02:13:48.900 --> 02:13:51.910
and are in that space and they're keep looping back

3701
02:13:51.910 --> 02:13:53.800
to this thing that they wanna do,

3702
02:13:53.800 --> 02:13:55.710
but they just can't get off the dime.

3703
02:13:55.710 --> 02:13:58.610
Like they're paralyzed by that first step

3704
02:13:58.610 --> 02:14:01.240
or they're in that state of mind where they feel like

3705
02:14:01.240 --> 02:14:04.360
they have to have it all figured out before they can begin.

3706
02:14:04.360 --> 02:14:07.440
Or they're just irrational about the level of risk

3707
02:14:07.440 --> 02:14:10.310
mitigation that's required before trying something hard.

3708
02:14:10.310 --> 02:14:13.880
So maybe we're probably getting near the end

3709
02:14:13.880 --> 02:14:14.713
of our time here.

3710
02:14:14.713 --> 02:14:19.670
Like I wanna leave people with some kind of tactical

3711
02:14:19.670 --> 02:14:23.300
inspiration around the beginning process

3712
02:14:23.300 --> 02:14:25.500
of like actualizing a dream.

3713
02:14:25.500 --> 02:14:27.580
Well, it all does begin with that first step.

3714
02:14:27.580 --> 02:14:29.960
And like, if you're scared to take the first step,

3715
02:14:29.960 --> 02:14:34.721
like get past that, take the first, because any big goal,

3716
02:14:34.721 --> 02:14:36.570
it's gonna require a lot of little steps to get there,

3717
02:14:36.570 --> 02:14:38.340
whether that be running across the United States,

3718
02:14:38.340 --> 02:14:40.710
starting a business, whatever it is.

3719
02:14:40.710 --> 02:14:43.010
And you got to chop it up into manageable pieces.

3720
02:14:43.010 --> 02:14:44.270
When I ran across the United States,

3721
02:14:44.270 --> 02:14:46.500
it was in five mile segments, every five miles.

3722
02:14:46.500 --> 02:14:48.801
I'd see my crew take on water, take on food.

3723
02:14:48.801 --> 02:14:50.770
And that's what I started focusing on.

3724
02:14:50.770 --> 02:14:53.690
I couldn't, when I left Huntington Beach,

3725
02:14:53.690 --> 02:14:56.430
think about New York, too big, too far,

3726
02:14:56.430 --> 02:14:58.800
some days I couldn't think about the end of the day,

3727
02:14:58.800 --> 02:15:00.380
but I could think about five miles.

3728
02:15:00.380 --> 02:15:04.120
And so it's just about one foot in front of the other.

3729
02:15:04.120 --> 02:15:05.720
Those obstacles are gonna come.

3730
02:15:05.720 --> 02:15:09.280
You'll get past them and just keep moving.

3731
02:15:09.280 --> 02:15:11.490
One foot in front of the other big goals

3732
02:15:11.490 --> 02:15:13.123
require a lot of little goals.

3733
02:15:14.390 --> 02:15:17.222
What are you up to next?

3734
02:15:17.222 --> 02:15:19.060
I'm gonna go home for a minute.

3735
02:15:19.060 --> 02:15:20.993
I think Shelly's gonna be happy to have me.

3736
02:15:22.470 --> 02:15:25.410
And then I've got a lot of challenges in my head

3737
02:15:25.410 --> 02:15:29.580
that are kind of swirling me and Hellah talking about one.

3738
02:15:29.580 --> 02:15:31.750
I would like next year to go back over to the UK.

3739
02:15:31.750 --> 02:15:33.980
There's a thing called Three Peaks Challenge.

3740
02:15:33.980 --> 02:15:37.990
Highest point in Wales, Scotland, and England.

3741
02:15:37.990 --> 02:15:40.350
If you traverse them by foot, it's about 500 miles

3742
02:15:40.350 --> 02:15:41.480
hit all the top of the peaks.

3743
02:15:41.480 --> 02:15:44.340
I'd like to set that or grab the FKC for that.

3744
02:15:44.340 --> 02:15:45.610
It's that the thing that Tony Riddle did.

3745
02:15:45.610 --> 02:15:47.530
He did it as well, yeah, he did barefoot,

3746
02:15:47.530 --> 02:15:48.670
which is funny, Tony.

3747
02:15:48.670 --> 02:15:49.503
Like we,-

3748
02:15:50.890 --> 02:15:51.723
I mean.

3749
02:15:51.723 --> 02:15:53.350
You know, we ran into him on the last day

3750
02:15:53.350 --> 02:15:55.000
of William's run.

3751
02:15:55.000 --> 02:15:57.530
William was coming south when he did joggle as well.

3752
02:15:57.530 --> 02:16:00.290
Tony did, he was going south to north.

3753
02:16:00.290 --> 02:16:03.250
We were coming north to south and on William's last day,

3754
02:16:03.250 --> 02:16:04.800
it was Tony's first day.

3755
02:16:04.800 --> 02:16:07.520
And we met up like on the road, just ran into each other.

3756
02:16:07.520 --> 02:16:09.580
And of course, all he did was look at our feet

3757
02:16:09.580 --> 02:16:11.240
and I had-

3758
02:16:11.240 --> 02:16:12.740
Judgment.-
(Rich laughing)

3759
02:16:12.740 --> 02:16:14.150
I had Luna sandals on at the moment.

3760
02:16:14.150 --> 02:16:15.170
He's like, "Good shoes."

3761
02:16:15.170 --> 02:16:17.690
I was like, but yeah.

3762
02:16:17.690 --> 02:16:19.330
So I would like to do that.

3763
02:16:19.330 --> 02:16:23.200
I signed up for the Cruel Jewel 100 it's in Georgia.

3764
02:16:23.200 --> 02:16:26.980
It's 100 mile race with 34,000 feet of gain.

3765
02:16:26.980 --> 02:16:29.670
The 50 miler version of that was my first big ultra.

3766
02:16:29.670 --> 02:16:31.460
So it's gonna be kind of like, I grew up in Georgia too.

3767
02:16:31.460 --> 02:16:35.770
So it's kind of like a going back to where it all started.

3768
02:16:35.770 --> 02:16:36.603
That's kind of the goal of that one.

3769
02:16:36.603 --> 02:16:38.230
Cool.

3770
02:16:38.230 --> 02:16:43.210
Are we in a place to open it up to questions, right now?

3771
02:16:43.210 --> 02:16:45.280
Rich has an amazing podcast.

3772
02:16:45.280 --> 02:16:47.890
What are you listening to, or what doing with your mind?

3773
02:16:47.890 --> 02:16:49.770
What are you doing over and over again?

3774
02:16:49.770 --> 02:16:54.770
Because that's essentially battling that mental, you know,

3775
02:16:55.960 --> 02:16:58.460
once you there, 'cause it's a lot to kind of deal with,

3776
02:16:58.460 --> 02:16:59.320
you know?

3777
02:16:59.320 --> 02:17:01.250
Great question.

3778
02:17:01.250 --> 02:17:02.746
Dan must have a podcast.

3779
02:17:02.746 --> 02:17:05.474
(Robbie laughing)

3780
02:17:05.474 --> 02:17:06.520
It goes a lot of places, you know,

3781
02:17:06.520 --> 02:17:09.573
it is listening to meaningful podcasts like Rich's.

3782
02:17:10.506 --> 02:17:12.540
I can't really do audio books.

3783
02:17:12.540 --> 02:17:14.770
Sometimes it's music when I'm in a big challenge,

3784
02:17:14.770 --> 02:17:17.280
what I've found is I fixate on a couple of songs

3785
02:17:17.280 --> 02:17:19.513
or artists and it just gets on repeat.

3786
02:17:21.130 --> 02:17:24.500
This summer was Tyler Childers, "Nose On The Grindstone"

3787
02:17:24.500 --> 02:17:26.047
I think that song, just the name of the song,

3788
02:17:26.047 --> 02:17:28.900
"Nose On The Grindstone," like it was where I was at.

3789
02:17:28.900 --> 02:17:32.840
And then a lot of it is me just going through my life,

3790
02:17:32.840 --> 02:17:34.160
that kind of free association.

3791
02:17:34.160 --> 02:17:35.610
But you know, a lot of this,

3792
02:17:35.610 --> 02:17:38.240
I remember on the Colorado Trail, it was like,

3793
02:17:38.240 --> 02:17:40.250
that was the hardest part of the summer physically.

3794
02:17:40.250 --> 02:17:43.230
And I would have those like things where it's like, oh,

3795
02:17:43.230 --> 02:17:44.750
you're in all these enlightened moments

3796
02:17:44.750 --> 02:17:46.557
of why I was doing it.

3797
02:17:46.557 --> 02:17:47.390
It was so, yeah, whatever.

3798
02:17:47.390 --> 02:17:48.474
And then I'd be like,

3799
02:17:48.474 --> 02:17:50.160
oh, it's just cause you're a bad motherfucker, you know?

3800
02:17:50.160 --> 02:17:52.660
And like, just stick with that one for a minute.

3801
02:17:52.660 --> 02:17:54.320
So yeah, it goes all over the place.

3802
02:17:54.320 --> 02:17:55.890
I can't do music all the time.

3803
02:17:55.890 --> 02:17:57.680
It gets to where it just overwhelms you.

3804
02:17:57.680 --> 02:17:59.277
I'd say probably 30% of the time

3805
02:17:59.277 --> 02:18:04.277
and the rest is sifting through these free association

3806
02:18:04.340 --> 02:18:06.610
thoughts and just coming up with new things,

3807
02:18:06.610 --> 02:18:08.180
I wanna do dreaming about the future,

3808
02:18:08.180 --> 02:18:11.250
whether that be running or the other parts of life

3809
02:18:11.250 --> 02:18:12.083
that really matter.

3810
02:18:12.083 --> 02:18:13.350
It's not all about running.

3811
02:18:14.290 --> 02:18:17.100
I just have a question about community.

3812
02:18:17.100 --> 02:18:22.100
So somebody who worked in nightlife and restaurant industry,

3813
02:18:22.390 --> 02:18:26.910
I know the communities work hard party even harder.

3814
02:18:26.910 --> 02:18:30.650
And then you decided to take an athletic route

3815
02:18:30.650 --> 02:18:32.894
and start running races and stuff like that.

3816
02:18:32.894 --> 02:18:36.380
So I'm sure over the years, your community has changed

3817
02:18:36.380 --> 02:18:41.110
from partying to healthier living, running,

3818
02:18:41.110 --> 02:18:42.883
athleticism in general.

3819
02:18:44.166 --> 02:18:44.999
How was that transition?

3820
02:18:44.999 --> 02:18:48.810
Did you experience kind of like loneliness in between

3821
02:18:48.810 --> 02:18:51.050
where you kind of left the party scene,

3822
02:18:51.050 --> 02:18:56.050
but you haven't built yet more healthy, different community?

3823
02:18:56.070 --> 02:18:57.400
Was it lonely at times?

3824
02:18:57.400 --> 02:18:59.170
Well, initially it wasn't too bad

3825
02:18:59.170 --> 02:19:01.150
because I was still in the restaurants.

3826
02:19:01.150 --> 02:19:05.290
I wasn't going out afterwards.

3827
02:19:05.290 --> 02:19:07.373
So I still had that community around me.

3828
02:19:08.450 --> 02:19:10.500
Really, I think we're more of the isolation came,

3829
02:19:10.500 --> 02:19:14.400
was we moved eventually from Austin up to Denver, Colorado,

3830
02:19:14.400 --> 02:19:15.970
and it was just Shelly and I,

3831
02:19:15.970 --> 02:19:18.356
and friendships were hard to come by.

3832
02:19:18.356 --> 02:19:20.120
As you get older, it's harder.

3833
02:19:20.120 --> 02:19:22.700
So it was a lot of just running alone.

3834
02:19:22.700 --> 02:19:25.190
But then you over time gradually have started to make

3835
02:19:25.190 --> 02:19:27.740
friends within this world, meaningful friendships,

3836
02:19:27.740 --> 02:19:32.740
like Hellah, like William, Rich and in with those it, yeah,

3837
02:19:33.680 --> 02:19:35.430
but there was, I think the easy to that is,

3838
02:19:35.430 --> 02:19:36.640
there's definitely been some of that.

3839
02:19:36.640 --> 02:19:39.060
There's been some lonely times in isolation,

3840
02:19:39.060 --> 02:19:41.210
but one of the things I love about these big challenges

3841
02:19:41.210 --> 02:19:44.070
that brings people together, I can not do these by myself.

3842
02:19:44.070 --> 02:19:46.790
And I'm really lucky to have an amazing support group

3843
02:19:46.790 --> 02:19:47.630
around me.

3844
02:19:47.630 --> 02:19:50.400
And what I love is it gives us this like real,

3845
02:19:50.400 --> 02:19:51.410
like time together.

3846
02:19:51.410 --> 02:19:54.900
It's very like concentrated and it's beautiful and fun.

3847
02:19:54.900 --> 02:19:58.470
So I think it's instead of like always having people around

3848
02:19:58.470 --> 02:20:00.310
being at the bar partying,

3849
02:20:00.310 --> 02:20:02.020
it's like I have a lot of alone time,

3850
02:20:02.020 --> 02:20:04.360
but then when I do have people around it's meaningful

3851
02:20:04.360 --> 02:20:06.423
and it's just concentrated good time.

3852
02:20:07.790 --> 02:20:10.280
I think, just to piggyback on that piece

3853
02:20:10.280 --> 02:20:14.440
a little bit, the comfort with the alone time comes

3854
02:20:14.440 --> 02:20:17.060
with getting comfortable with who you are, right?

3855
02:20:17.060 --> 02:20:19.260
Like if you're uncomfortable with who you are

3856
02:20:19.260 --> 02:20:21.500
and you've been spending a lot of your time

3857
02:20:21.500 --> 02:20:24.860
with, you know, in recovery parlance,

3858
02:20:24.860 --> 02:20:26.250
it's called lower companions.

3859
02:20:26.250 --> 02:20:27.950
Like you're, you know, you're out partying

3860
02:20:27.950 --> 02:20:30.130
and then you wanna switch up your life.

3861
02:20:30.130 --> 02:20:33.137
You wanna be sober, or you wanna invest your time

3862
02:20:33.137 --> 02:20:35.780
and your energy and healthier pursuits.

3863
02:20:35.780 --> 02:20:39.190
It does require finding a new community of people

3864
02:20:39.190 --> 02:20:41.550
to surround yourself with who are vibrating

3865
02:20:41.550 --> 02:20:44.660
at a higher level and are kind of a little bit further down

3866
02:20:44.660 --> 02:20:47.470
the path of where you wanna go.

3867
02:20:47.470 --> 02:20:51.100
And along that journey is the inside work of, you know,

3868
02:20:51.100 --> 02:20:54.630
reckoning with whatever was leading you down the darker path

3869
02:20:54.630 --> 02:20:56.580
and clearing that out and getting comfortable

3870
02:20:56.580 --> 02:20:59.040
with who you are so that when you are alone,

3871
02:20:59.040 --> 02:21:01.640
you're not feeling less than,

3872
02:21:01.640 --> 02:21:03.570
but you're actually comfortable with who you are

3873
02:21:03.570 --> 02:21:06.480
because you'd like the person that you are becoming.

3874
02:21:06.480 --> 02:21:09.258
And the great news is for anybody who lives

3875
02:21:09.258 --> 02:21:10.091
in this amazing city,

3876
02:21:10.091 --> 02:21:13.170
there is every possible community of people doing any number

3877
02:21:13.170 --> 02:21:17.080
of things that are available to you as a person

3878
02:21:17.080 --> 02:21:18.820
who's, you know, trying out new things

3879
02:21:18.820 --> 02:21:20.910
and trying to figure out where you fit in.

3880
02:21:20.910 --> 02:21:23.490
You seem to have been touched deeply

3881
02:21:23.490 --> 02:21:24.730
by the Navajo Region.

3882
02:21:24.730 --> 02:21:26.780
I'm just wondering if you can tell us a little bit about,

3883
02:21:26.780 --> 02:21:31.370
about that area and why it moved you so greatly.

3884
02:21:31.370 --> 02:21:32.490
The people.

3885
02:21:32.490 --> 02:21:35.100
It's just, it's like, as I ran across the U.S.

3886
02:21:35.100 --> 02:21:38.210
like not a lot of people ask what you're doing

3887
02:21:38.210 --> 02:21:40.190
and if they do, and you're like,

3888
02:21:40.190 --> 02:21:41.127
I'm running across U.S. and they say,

3889
02:21:41.127 --> 02:21:41.970
"Oh, okay."

3890
02:21:41.970 --> 02:21:44.145
Back on their phone. And you're like,

3891
02:21:44.145 --> 02:21:46.150
"Did you not hear what I just said?"

3892
02:21:46.150 --> 02:21:48.229
And in the Navajo Nation,

3893
02:21:48.229 --> 02:21:50.350
almost every car stopped and they wanted to know

3894
02:21:50.350 --> 02:21:51.183
what I was doing.

3895
02:21:51.183 --> 02:21:53.190
And they were proud for me to be,

3896
02:21:53.190 --> 02:21:56.010
have chosen to come through their land.

3897
02:21:56.010 --> 02:21:59.150
And that was just, it was touching.

3898
02:21:59.150 --> 02:22:02.230
They look you in the eye, they want to have conversation

3899
02:22:02.230 --> 02:22:05.070
and they're proud of what they have

3900
02:22:05.070 --> 02:22:06.920
and what they have isn't a lot.

3901
02:22:06.920 --> 02:22:10.990
And that really makes you feel really grateful

3902
02:22:10.990 --> 02:22:12.190
for the things you do have,

3903
02:22:12.190 --> 02:22:14.730
and also remind you to be grateful for what you have

3904
02:22:14.730 --> 02:22:17.980
and understand that it's, again,

3905
02:22:17.980 --> 02:22:20.350
it's all mindset and they just have a culture that's set up

3906
02:22:20.350 --> 02:22:23.360
in a way that they stay true to themselves

3907
02:22:23.360 --> 02:22:25.627
and they they're prideful in who they are.

3908
02:22:25.627 --> 02:22:27.740
And that really resonated with me.

3909
02:22:27.740 --> 02:22:29.670
Yeah. Hi, I have a question, Robbie,

3910
02:22:29.670 --> 02:22:33.440
can you talk about how you recover in-between your running?

3911
02:22:33.440 --> 02:22:35.310
No, because I know you used like hardware

3912
02:22:35.310 --> 02:22:38.310
and software tools, like both of your endurance

3913
02:22:38.310 --> 02:22:40.440
and also your mental state and that.

3914
02:22:40.440 --> 02:22:44.040
Yeah. One thing I realized this summer

3915
02:22:44.040 --> 02:22:49.040
was I think I often on runs like start to acknowledge

3916
02:22:50.510 --> 02:22:52.870
and define things and then I can explore them further.

3917
02:22:52.870 --> 02:22:54.700
And I realized that the pursuit

3918
02:22:54.700 --> 02:22:57.530
I'm on is one of trying to understand

3919
02:22:57.530 --> 02:23:00.370
how to sustainably move through space and time.

3920
02:23:00.370 --> 02:23:02.440
That's like the real way to say it.

3921
02:23:02.440 --> 02:23:04.760
And it's really just like how to find sustainable motion.

3922
02:23:04.760 --> 02:23:08.350
And I thought a lot about like native American cultures

3923
02:23:08.350 --> 02:23:12.750
and how before horses were around, they had to move by foot

3924
02:23:12.750 --> 02:23:15.440
and how they must've been so valued in society.

3925
02:23:15.440 --> 02:23:18.340
And so looking back when I ran across the United States,

3926
02:23:18.340 --> 02:23:19.860
that was all about my food choices.

3927
02:23:19.860 --> 02:23:21.060
And I thought if I went plant based,

3928
02:23:21.060 --> 02:23:22.290
that was the key to success

3929
02:23:22.290 --> 02:23:24.703
and I would be able to run indefinitely.

3930
02:23:25.670 --> 02:23:27.605
And then when this summer came around,

3931
02:23:27.605 --> 02:23:29.180
I realized through the Transcon,

3932
02:23:29.180 --> 02:23:31.350
there was a piece that I had missed.

3933
02:23:31.350 --> 02:23:33.530
And that piece I had missed was recovery.

3934
02:23:33.530 --> 02:23:35.180
I had no recovery plan.

3935
02:23:35.180 --> 02:23:37.290
And due to that, I wasn't sleeping a lot.

3936
02:23:37.290 --> 02:23:38.480
There were days that were a lot harder

3937
02:23:38.480 --> 02:23:39.313
than they needed to be.

3938
02:23:39.313 --> 02:23:42.130
I was having to dig in ways that had I gotten good rest,

3939
02:23:42.130 --> 02:23:43.190
wouldn't be there.

3940
02:23:43.190 --> 02:23:45.250
So recovery became the focal point.

3941
02:23:45.250 --> 02:23:48.410
And with that, one of my sponsors, we did the summer,

3942
02:23:48.410 --> 02:23:50.235
it was a Ten Thousand and then NuCalm,

3943
02:23:50.235 --> 02:23:52.160
and NuCalm is a neuroscience technology

3944
02:23:52.160 --> 02:23:53.600
that through a three-part system,

3945
02:23:53.600 --> 02:23:55.880
it brings your brain into alpha and theta waves.

3946
02:23:55.880 --> 02:23:59.370
And lets you get really meaningful rest when you need it.

3947
02:23:59.370 --> 02:24:03.280
And a lot of this summer was exploring that as a recovery

3948
02:24:03.280 --> 02:24:05.620
tool, and if it would really help me along the way

3949
02:24:05.620 --> 02:24:08.380
and the truth of it was is it was one of the pillars

3950
02:24:08.380 --> 02:24:10.760
of my success and then fast forwarding.

3951
02:24:10.760 --> 02:24:12.630
I went and helped William Goodge in the UK.

3952
02:24:12.630 --> 02:24:16.080
He also utilizes NuCalm and he used it in between

3953
02:24:16.080 --> 02:24:16.990
his marathons.

3954
02:24:16.990 --> 02:24:19.110
So essentially we talked a lot about his run

3955
02:24:19.110 --> 02:24:21.090
and it was 30 days, 48 marathons.

3956
02:24:21.090 --> 02:24:21.923
But he said,

3957
02:24:21.923 --> 02:24:24.240
because he would take a nap in between and use this

3958
02:24:24.240 --> 02:24:25.910
for him it was 48 days.

3959
02:24:25.910 --> 02:24:28.490
Like he got enough restful sleep in between

3960
02:24:28.490 --> 02:24:29.650
to take on the next day.

3961
02:24:29.650 --> 02:24:32.790
So that was kind of the thesis of this summer, recovery.

3962
02:24:32.790 --> 02:24:35.630
And that was the tool that I used to do it.

3963
02:24:35.630 --> 02:24:36.650
What I learned from William

3964
02:24:36.650 --> 02:24:38.700
also was he's like really strong.

3965
02:24:38.700 --> 02:24:42.220
He's muscular and that's my next one.

3966
02:24:42.220 --> 02:24:45.920
I've got to get more full body like a full body workout

3967
02:24:45.920 --> 02:24:46.753
going, which is good.

3968
02:24:46.753 --> 02:24:48.540
'Cause Ten Thousand has all the gear for that.

3969
02:24:48.540 --> 02:24:51.550
And maybe you can follow that journey

3970
02:24:51.550 --> 02:24:53.330
as I kind of get biceps and stuff.

3971
02:24:53.330 --> 02:24:54.251
We'll see.

3972
02:24:54.251 --> 02:24:56.330
(all laughing)

3973
02:24:56.330 --> 02:24:58.720
So I just had a question for as often as you guys

3974
02:24:58.720 --> 02:25:00.720
obviously train and I would love to hear from both of you

3975
02:25:00.720 --> 02:25:01.850
guys on this, honestly, for,

3976
02:25:01.850 --> 02:25:05.810
as often as you train as competitive as you guys are for as

3977
02:25:05.810 --> 02:25:09.120
long as you've been going, how is your why like evolved.

3978
02:25:09.120 --> 02:25:11.780
'Cause I'm sure the reason of why you started running

3979
02:25:11.780 --> 02:25:14.570
at this high level through the years has changed.

3980
02:25:14.570 --> 02:25:16.670
And I'm just curious how you kept like the fire burning,

3981
02:25:16.670 --> 02:25:18.620
like how you kept the competitive edge.

3982
02:25:19.699 --> 02:25:21.810
Yeah. I mean, for me is when we talk

3983
02:25:21.810 --> 02:25:22.700
about the big stuff,

3984
02:25:22.700 --> 02:25:24.630
like starting with the Transcontinental run,

3985
02:25:24.630 --> 02:25:26.880
the why there was con was very clear.

3986
02:25:26.880 --> 02:25:28.810
It was to promote a plant based diet.

3987
02:25:28.810 --> 02:25:32.420
I had a reckoning where I wanted to do something

3988
02:25:32.420 --> 02:25:34.760
that could help like the environmental fight.

3989
02:25:34.760 --> 02:25:37.220
And I found that a plant-based diet is a thing

3990
02:25:37.220 --> 02:25:38.717
that we as individuals can do.

3991
02:25:38.717 --> 02:25:40.490
And I knew it was good for my performance.

3992
02:25:40.490 --> 02:25:44.110
And I wanted to prove that and boast about this diet

3993
02:25:44.110 --> 02:25:47.120
and that's still like a core part of me,

3994
02:25:47.120 --> 02:25:48.500
but I did that work, right.

3995
02:25:48.500 --> 02:25:50.930
Like probably more than most people I ran across the country

3996
02:25:50.930 --> 02:25:51.763
to talk about it.

3997
02:25:51.763 --> 02:25:52.890
So it's like there.

3998
02:25:52.890 --> 02:25:55.260
And so the why now what I realized on that run

3999
02:25:55.260 --> 02:25:57.930
was I was also able to inspire people in other ways.

4000
02:25:57.930 --> 02:25:59.930
It wasn't all about the plant-based diet.

4001
02:25:59.930 --> 02:26:03.780
And knowing that by my efforts could encourage someone else

4002
02:26:03.780 --> 02:26:05.000
to pick up better habits,

4003
02:26:05.000 --> 02:26:06.930
like giving somebody the gift of running.

4004
02:26:06.930 --> 02:26:09.310
There's no better gift anyone's ever given me.

4005
02:26:09.310 --> 02:26:10.963
And so that keeps me going.

4006
02:26:12.600 --> 02:26:14.830
Yeah, my evolution is, is somewhat similar.

4007
02:26:14.830 --> 02:26:18.990
I mean, early on when I was heavily invested in training

4008
02:26:18.990 --> 02:26:20.670
and racing and ultra races,

4009
02:26:20.670 --> 02:26:23.720
